### 3. Important: What should Git track?

| File/folder | Git tracking |
|---|---|
| `app/` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `tests/` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `alembic/versions/` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `alembic/env.py` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `.env.example` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `.gitignore` | <badge color="success">Track</badge> |
| `studentenv/` | <badge color="danger">Ignore</badge> |
| `.env` | <badge color="danger">Ignore</badge> |
| `*.db` | <badge color="danger">Ignore</badge> |
| `__pycache__/` | <badge color="danger">Ignore</badge> |
| `.pytest_cache/` | <badge color="danger">Ignore</badge> |

**Important:** Never ignore the Alembic migration scripts themselves. They are essential for reproducing your database schema.

### 4. Verify your `.gitignore`

Save the file and run these commands from your project root:

```powershell
git check-ignore studentenv/
git check-ignore .pytest_cache/
git check-ignore student_management.db
git check-ignore .env
```

If those files exist, Git should report their ignored paths.

Now check:

```powershell
git status
```

You should see your application source files and migrations available for tracking, but not your ignored files.

<badge color="success">GitHub preparation</badge>

